}
}

\subtree{
\title{9. Not established, not done}

\ul{
\li{\strong{\code{\verb~|nondep~} is declined in the strict mode, not represented.} The exact solution (the flag as part of a LET node's identity: LP2's design of 22:05Z - a header bit that \code{\verb~|Overlay.mkLet~} hashes, a tag bit of the block record, a second letE tag of the packed runs, every rebuild of a LET in \code{\verb~|TermOps~}, the machine, the admission) is not started: it changes the store format and six modules for a flag that no letE record of the corpora or tests has, and the decline costs nothing there.}
\li{\strong{\code{\verb~|mdata~} and \code{\verb~|nondep~} are declined, not represented}: a strict run of a file with them gives no verdict. An export of real code written with \code{\verb~|--export-mdata~} is such a file.}
\li{\strong{Admission in the strict mode is fresher than the official kernel in three places} (D6): the four judgments per constructor of \code{\verb~|check_recursors~}, the nested-application checks and the final re-check share one official checker; the strict kernel gives each judgment empty caches. Whether RLVM's admission asks those judgments at all in the official order is the admission's matter (finding L-F1 of today is in the same code). Other divergences of the admission from \code{\verb~|add_inductive~} were not audited here.}
\li{\strong{The second pass inside admission}: an object-identity hazard in a judgment asked through \code{\verb~|KernelOps~} is declined at once in the strict mode (no session to check both ways). Not seen on the corpora.}
\li{\strong{\code{\verb~|sharecommon~}} (\code{\verb~|add_theorem~} shares the value and the type before checking) was read in the source, not shown by an export: every construction of a hazard I have needs a term that lazy delta produces from ANOTHER declaration's value, which \code{\verb~|sharecommon~} never touches.}
\li{\strong{No export for D4, D5, D7.} For D4 and D7 I could not construct a verdict that depends on them (the argument is in section 4); they are corrected because the official tables are what they are. D5 is a matter of thread timing.}
\li{\strong{The both-ways check assumes that the two continuations at a hazard are the only official behaviours} and that everything else in the kernel is the official procedure. The evidence for the first is section 3's table (each continuation reproduces the official verdict exactly where it is the truth); for the second, the gates. A hazard is decided by at most 63 checks of the declaration; more would be a decline (not seen).}
\li{\strong{The safety net of section 6.1 covers metadata only.} A wrong reject of the practical mode on a file WITHOUT a metadata record is outside it (none is known: the hostile exports have no "official accept, rejected" row); the \code{\verb~|nondep~} flag does not trigger it (no reject on such a file exists in the sets). A correct reject of a file with a node becomes a decline wherever the strict load declines a declaration before the rejected one (351 of 485 such mutants). The net's second run uses the heap of the first (the JVM launcher sizes it by the default engine).}
\li{The rule of section 6.1 fails closed in a file that names \code{\verb~|eagerReduce~}: there a metadata node around ANY record of an earlier declaration (or of a packed run) declines the declaration, because the loader cannot read such a record back.}
\li{Mathlib: see section 7 for what was run.}
}
}
